            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Iron Ore Mineralogy: Types, Grades & Chemical Properties</h3>\n            <p><strong>Iron ore</strong> is the bedrock of modern industrial civilization and the indispensable raw material for the iron and steel industry. Iron occurs naturally in several mineral forms, distinguished by their metallic iron content ($Fe$), chemical composition, color, and magnetic characteristics.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Major Varieties of Iron Ore</h4>\n            <table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 10px; width: 100%;\">\n                <tr style=\"background-color: #f0f0f0;\">\n                    <th>Ore Type</th>\n                    <th>Chemical Formula</th>\n                    <th>Metallic Content ($Fe$)</th>\n                    <th>Color & Diagnostic Properties</th>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>Magnetite</strong></td>\n                    <td>$Fe_3O_4$</td>\n                    <td><strong>Up to 72.4%</strong> (Highest Grade)</td>\n                    <td>Black to brownish-black; strongly magnetic with natural polarity (lodestone). Excellent electrical and magnetic properties. Occurs in metamorphic Banded Magnetite Quartzite (BMQ) formations.</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>Hematite</strong></td>\n                    <td>$Fe_2O_3$</td>\n                    <td><strong>60% to 70%</strong> (Prime Industrial Ore)</td>\n                    <td>Reddish-brown to steel-gray; leaves a distinctive cherry-red streak. Known as 'red ore'. <strong>Accounts for over 80% of India's total iron ore production</strong> and steel plant consumption.</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>Limonite</strong></td>\n                    <td>$2Fe_2O_3 \\cdot 3H_2O$</td>\n                    <td><strong>35% to 50%</strong> (Hydrated Ore)</td>\n                    <td>Yellow to yellowish-brown. Formed through weathering and hydration of other iron minerals (gossan caps and lateritic horizons).</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>Siderite</strong></td>\n                    <td>$FeCO_3$</td>\n                    <td><strong>Less than 40%</strong> (Carbonate Ore)</td>\n                    <td>Ash-gray to brown; contains carbon dioxide. Self-fluxing during smelting due to carbonate content, but low iron tenor limits commercial viability.</td>\n                </tr>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4>2. Banded Iron Formations (BIF)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Almost all major Indian hematite and magnetite deposits occur in Precambrian <strong>Banded Iron Formations (BIF)</strong>—specifically Banded Hematite Quartzite (BHQ) and Banded Hematite Jasper (BHJ)—deposited in marine basins between 2.5 and 3.2 billion years ago during the Great Oxidation Event.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
