            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Iron Ore Reserves of India: State-Wise Rankings & Resource Endowments</h3>\n            <p>India is endowed with abundant, world-class deposits of iron ore, comprising both high-grade hematite and vast resources of magnetite. According to official geological inventories and the National Mineral Data Yearbook, India’s iron ore reserves display marked spatial concentration.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Leading States in Total Iron Ore Reserves</h4>\n            <p>In terms of total in-situ geological reserves (Hematite and Magnetite combined), the official state hierarchy is:</p>\n            <div style=\"background:#f1f5f9; padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-weight:700; color:#1e293b; text-align:center; margin:12px 0;\">\n                Karnataka &gt; Odisha &gt; Jharkhand &gt; Chhattisgarh\n            </div>\n\n            <h4>2. State-Wise Geological Endowments</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Karnataka (Leader in Total Reserves):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Karnataka possesses the largest total iron ore reserves in the country, anchored by massive deposits of <strong>Magnetite</strong> along the Western Ghats (Chikmagalur, Kudremukh, Bababudan Hills) and high-grade <strong>Hematite</strong> in the Sandur-Hospet-Bellary belt.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>2. Odisha (Second in Reserves, Leader in Hematite):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Houses the country's most valuable, readily exploitable high-grade <strong>Hematite reserves</strong> (accounting for over 33% of India's total hematite resources), concentrated in the iron ore triangle of Keonjhar, Mayurbhanj, and Sundargarh districts.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>3. Jharkhand (Third in Total Reserves):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Features the historic iron ore ranges of West Singhbhum (Noamundi, Gua, Kiriburu), which ignited India’s modern steel industry under J.N. Tata at Jamshedpur.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>4. Chhattisgarh (Fourth in Total Reserves):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Possesses massive, super-high-grade hematite reserves in the Bailadila range (Dantewada district) and Dalli-Rajhara (Durg district).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Hematite vs Magnetite Spatial Divide</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Hematite Resources:</strong> Over 75% of India’s high-grade hematite reserves are concentrated in the eastern belt: <strong>Odisha, Jharkhand, and Chhattisgarh</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Magnetite Resources:</strong> Over 75% of India’s magnetite resources are located in southern India, predominantly in <strong>Karnataka</strong> (Bababudan Hills, Kudremukh), Andhra Pradesh, and Tamil Nadu.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
