            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Iron Ore Production in India: State-Wise Rankings & Spatial Hegemony</h3>\n            <p>India is one of the world's leading producers of iron ore. Unlike reserves (which reflect in-situ geological deposits), <strong>production</strong> measures the actual annual volume of ore extracted, beneficiated, and dispatched from operating mines.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Leading States in Iron Ore Production</h4>\n            <p>According to the latest Indian Minerals Yearbook and official Ministry of Mines production data, the descending order of iron ore producing states in India is:</p>\n            <div style=\"background:#f1f5f9; padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-weight:700; color:#1e293b; text-align:center; margin:12px 0;\">\n                Odisha &gt; Chhattisgarh &gt; Karnataka &gt; Jharkhand\n            </div>\n\n            <h4>2. Breakdown of Major Producing States</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Odisha (Dominant Producer, &gt;50% of National Output):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Odisha is the undisputed powerhouse of Indian iron ore mining, consistently contributing <strong>more than 50% to 55% of India's total annual iron ore production</strong> (over 140 million tonnes annually).</li>\n                        <li>Key Production Centers: Joda, Barbil, Koira, and Thakurani in Keonjhar; Gorumahisani and Badampahar in Mayurbhanj; and the Bonai iron range in Sundargarh.</li>\n                        <li>Captive & Merchant Mines: Operated by major corporations including Tata Steel, SAIL, Odisha Mining Corporation (OMC), and Jindal Steel & Power.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>2. Chhattisgarh (Second Largest Producer, ~16%–18%):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Anchored by the massive, ultra-mechanized open-pit operations of the <strong>National Mineral Development Corporation (NMDC) at Bailadila</strong> (Complex 14, 11C, Deposit 5). Bailadila produces exceptional lump ore ($Fe \\ge 66\\%$).</li>\n                        <li>Dalli-Rajhara mines operated by SAIL as captive feedstock for the <strong>Bhilai Steel Plant</strong>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>3. Karnataka (Third Largest Producer, ~13%–15%):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Centered in the Bellary-Hospet-Sandur iron ore belt, operated largely under strict Supreme Court environmental monitoring and e-auction frameworks (NMDC Donimalai and Kumaraswamy mines).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>4. Jharkhand (Fourth Largest Producer, ~10%–11%):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Concentrated in the Kolhan division of West Singhbhum (Noamundi, Gua, Chiria, Meghahatuburu), feeding SAIL and Tata Steel plants.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Critical Contrast: Reserves vs Production</h4>\n            <div style=\"background:#fffbeb; border:1px solid #fde68a; padding:12px; border-radius:8px; margin-top:10px;\">\n                <strong>Key Examination Distinction:</strong>\n                <br>• <strong>Reserves Ranking:</strong> Karnataka &gt; Odisha &gt; Jharkhand &gt; Chhattisgarh\n                <br>• <strong>Production Ranking:</strong> Odisha &gt; Chhattisgarh &gt; Karnataka &gt; Jharkhand\n                <br><em>Why?</em> Karnataka leads in reserves due to Western Ghats magnetite deposits that remain largely unmined due to ecological restrictions, whereas Odisha leads in active production due to extensive mechanized hematite mining in Keonjhar.\n            </div>\n        ",
