            "title": "Iron Ore Global Comparisons: World Reserves, Production Rankings & Major Mining Belts",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Iron Ore Global Landscape: Reserves, Production Rankings & Mining Basins</h3>\n            <p>Iron ore is the world's most heavily mined and internationally traded metal commodity. Global production and pricing are anchored by giant open-pit mining operations across Australia, Brazil, China, and India, with seaborne trade centered on bulk carriers sailing to Asian blast furnaces.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Global Iron Ore Reserves Ranking</h4>\n            <p>According to the United States Geological Survey (USGS) and international mineral databases, the leading nations in total iron ore reserves (crude ore) are:</p>\n            <div style=\"background:#f1f5f9; padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-weight:700; color:#1e293b; text-align:center; margin:12px 0;\">\n                Australia &gt; Brazil &gt; Russia &gt; China &gt; India\n            </div>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Australia (1st in Reserves):</strong> Holds over 50 billion tonnes of crude ore, concentrated in the giant banded iron formations of the <strong>Pilbara Craton</strong> (Western Australia).</li>\n                <li><strong>Brazil (2nd in Reserves):</strong> Famous for the world's purest hematite deposits in the <strong>Carajás mine</strong> (Pará state) and the 'Iron Quadrangle' (<em>Quadrilátero Ferrífero</em> in Minas Gerais).</li>\n                <li><strong>Russia (3rd in Reserves):</strong> Massive reserves anchored by the <strong>Kursk Magnetic Anomaly (KMA)</strong>, one of the largest magnetic anomalies on Earth.</li>\n                <li><strong>India (5th in Reserves):</strong> Holds approximately 34 billion tonnes of hematite and magnetite resources across the Peninsular shield.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Global Iron Ore Production Ranking</h4>\n            <p>In terms of annual mined production volume, the official global hierarchy is:</p>\n            <div style=\"background:#f1f5f9; padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-weight:700; color:#1e293b; text-align:center; margin:12px 0;\">\n                Australia &gt; Brazil &gt; India &gt; China\n            </div>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Australia:</strong> Dominates global seaborne supply, producing over 900 million tonnes annually (operated by Rio Tinto, BHP, and Fortescue).</li>\n                <li><strong>2. Brazil:</strong> Produces over 400 million tonnes annually (anchored by Vale S.A.).</li>\n                <li><strong>3. India:</strong> Produces over 250 to 280 million tonnes annually, solidly occupying the <strong>3rd position globally</strong> in iron ore production!</li>\n                <li><strong>4. China:</strong> Mined output is vast in gross crude tonnage, but Chinese ores have a very low average tenor (typically 20% to 30% Fe), requiring heavy beneficiation. On a usable iron-content basis, India surpasses China's domestic output.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Global Trade Dynamics</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>China as the Supreme Consumer:</strong> China consumes over 70% of global seaborne iron ore imports, feeding its massive 1-billion-tonne annual crude steel production.</li>\n                <li><strong>Seaborne Super-Routes:</strong> Pilbara to Qingdao (Australia-China) and Tubarão to Asian ports via giant Valemax bulk carriers (Brazil-China).</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
