            "title": "Major Iron Ore Belts of Odisha: Mayurbhanj, Keonjhar & Sundargarh Districts",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Major Iron Ore Belts of Odisha: Mayurbhanj, Keonjhar & Sundargarh</h3>\n            <p>Odisha is the heart of India's iron and steel economy, producing over 50% of the nation's total iron ore. The state's hematite deposits are hosted within the famous <strong>Iron Ore Group (IOG)</strong> of rocks forming the 'horse-shoe synclinorium' across Mayurbhanj, Keonjhar, and Sundargarh districts.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Mayurbhanj District: The Historic Cradle</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Historic Significance:</strong> Pramatha Nath Bose (P.N. Bose), the pioneering Indian geologist of the GSI, surveyed the Gorumahisani hills in 1904 and alerted J.N. Tata, leading directly to the founding of Tata Iron and Steel Company (TISCO) at Jamshedpur in 1907.</li>\n                <li><strong>Major Mining Hills:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Gorumahisani (Gurumahisani):</strong> Massive hematite ridge (63%–64% Fe).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Badampahar:</strong> High-grade hematite with low phosphorus.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Sulaipat:</strong> Known for exceptionally pure, dense lump ore.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Keonjhar (Kendujhar) District: The Production Epicenter</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Keonjhar is the single richest iron-ore-producing district in India, centered along the Barbil-Joda basin.</li>\n                <li><strong>Major Mines and Hills:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Kiriburu & Meghahatuburu:</strong> Giant mechanized mines straddling the Odisha-Jharkhand border; Kiriburu was developed initially in 1964 to export ore to Japan, later integrated as captive feed for the <strong>Bokaro Steel Plant</strong>.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Joda East & Joda West:</strong> Operated by Tata Steel; world-class mechanized mining with advanced wet beneficiation plants.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Thakurani, Bolani & Gandhamardan:</strong> Expansive mining leases supplying SAIL steel plants and domestic sponge iron producers.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Sundargarh District: The Western Flank</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Bonai Iron Range:</strong> Forms the western arm of the horse-shoe synclinorium.</li>\n                <li><strong>Barsuan & Kalta Mines:</strong> Captive mines of SAIL developed in the 1950s to directly feed the <strong>Rourkela Steel Plant (RSP)</strong>, India’s first public sector integrated steel plant built with German collaboration.</li>\n                <li><strong>Kasia & Khandadhar Hills:</strong> Prominent hematite ridges hosting both private and state mining operations.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. Industrial Ecosystem</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>The Mayurbhanj-Keonjhar-Sundargarh iron ore complex feeds the premier steel hub of eastern India: Rourkela, Jamshedpur, Kalinganagar (Tata Steel and Jindal), and Durgapur/Bokaro, with export flows evacuated through <strong>Paradip Port</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
