            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Major Iron Ore Belts of Karnataka: Western Ghats & The Bellary Basin</h3>\n            <p>Karnataka possesses the largest total in-situ geological reserves of iron ore in India. The state displays a remarkable geological duality: massive, unmined <strong>Magnetite</strong> formations embedded in the ecologically fragile Western Ghats (Chikmagalur), contrasted against intensive open-cast <strong>Hematite</strong> mining in the dry Deccan interior (Bellary-Hospet-Sandur belt).</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Chikmagalur District: The Western Ghats Magnetite Belts (Direct PDF Data)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Bababudan Hills (Kemmangundi):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Historic iron ore range where Sir M. Visvesvaraya established India's first southern steel works in 1923—the <strong>Visvesvaraya Iron and Steel Limited (VISL) at Bhadravati</strong> in Shimoga.</li>\n                        <li>Extensive Banded Magnetite Quartzite (BMQ) and hematite capping in the Kemmangundi ridges.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Kudremukh ('Horse-Face'):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Located in the Western Ghats of Chikmagalur. The mountain peak resembles a <em>horse's face</em> (Kudre = horse; Mukh = face).</li>\n                        <li>Developed in the 1970s as a 100% export-oriented open-cast magnetite project under the <strong>Kudremukh Iron Ore Company Limited (KIOCL)</strong>, funded with Iranian investment to supply Iranian steel mills.</li>\n                        <li><strong>The 110-km Slurry Pipeline:</strong> In an engineering feat, crushed magnetite ore was mixed with water into slurry and pumped through a <strong>110 km underground steel pipeline</strong> across the Western Ghats to Panambur (New Mangalore Port), where a pellet plant operated.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Supreme Court Environmental Ban:</strong> In 2005, the Supreme Court of India ordered the permanent closure of Kudremukh mining to protect the Kudremukh National Park, tropical rainforests, and the Bhadra river watershed from catastrophic siltation.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Bellary-Hospet-Sandur Belt: The Hematite Heartland</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Located in the eastern dry belt of Karnataka (Ballari and Vijayanagara districts).</li>\n                <li>Hosts high-grade hematite deposits (63% to 66% Fe) in the Sandur schist belt:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Donimalai & Kumaraswamy:</strong> State-of-the-art mechanized mines operated by NMDC.</li>\n                        <li>Feeds the colossal <strong>JSW Steel Vijayanagar works at Toranagallu</strong> (one of the largest single-location steel plants in the world).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Chitradurga, Tumkur & Shimoga Belts</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Additional iron-bearing formations in the Chitradurga and Tumkur districts of the central Dharwar craton.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
