            "id": "m09",
            "title": "Other Iron Ore Producing Regions: Goa, Maharashtra, MP & Andhra Pradesh",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Other Iron Ore Producing Regions: Goa, Maharashtra, MP & Andhra Pradesh</h3>\n            <p>Beyond the primary quadri-state complex (Odisha, Chhattisgarh, Karnataka, and Jharkhand), significant commercial and localized deposits of iron ore are distributed across Goa, Maharashtra, Madhya Pradesh, Andhra Pradesh, and Rajasthan.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Goa: The Coastal Export Pioneer (Direct PDF Data)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Geographical Occurrence:</strong> Concentrated in North Goa (Bicholim, Sanquelim, Adulmale, Kudnam) and South Goa (Sanguem, Quepem).</li>\n                <li><strong>Mineral Characteristics:</strong> Moderate to low-grade hematite, limonite, and goethite ($Fe \u0007pprox 55\\%–60\\%$). Due to high moisture and friability, Goan ore is processed into concentrates and pellets.</li>\n                <li><strong>Logistical Supremacy & Mormugao Port:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Goa possesses the unique logistical advantage of navigable river waterways (<strong>Mandovi and Zuari rivers</strong>).</li>\n                        <li>Ore is loaded directly onto river barges at mine-heads and ferried downstream to <strong>Mormugao Port</strong> for automated mechanical transshipment into ocean-going bulk freighters bound for China and Japan.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Maharashtra: Vidarbha & Konkan Belts (Direct PDF Data)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Eastern Vidarbha Belt:</strong> Hosted in Archean Dharwar rocks in <strong>Chandrapur, Gadchiroli, Nagpur, and Bhandara districts</strong>.\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><em>Surjagarh (Gadchiroli):</em> Giant high-grade hematite deposit currently being developed into a major mining and steel hub by Lloyds Metals.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Konkan Coastal Belt:</strong> Lateritic iron ore and titaniferous magnetite in <strong>Ratnagiri and Sindhudurg districts</strong>, utilizing coastal sea routes for evacuation.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Madhya Pradesh: The Central Ridges (Direct PDF Data)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Dispersed hematite and banded iron deposits across <strong>Balaghat, Chhindwara, Jabalpur, and Jhabua districts</strong>.</li>\n                <li>Jabalpur ore is historically utilized in local foundry, paint pigment, and cement flux applications.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. Andhra Pradesh: The Cuddapah & Coastal Formations (Direct PDF Data)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Ongole (Prakasam District) & Guntur District:</strong> Extensive Banded Magnetite Quartzite (BMQ) formations.</li>\n                <li><strong>Anantapur & Kurnool:</strong> Hematite deposits in Cuddapah synclinal formations historically mined along the Karnataka border.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>5. Rajasthan: North-Western Deposits</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Deposits at Morija-Banol (Jaipur), Neemla-Raisalo (Dausa), and Nathra-ki-Pal (Udaipur).</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
