            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Chromite: The Strategic Element of Stainless Steel & Refractories</h3>\n            <p><strong>Chromite</strong> ($FeCr_2O_4$) is an iron chromium oxide mineral and the <strong>sole commercial ore of chromium</strong> on Earth. Chromium is classified globally as a high-priority strategic mineral because of its irreplaceable metallurgical role in manufacturing stainless steel and high-temperature furnace linings.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Chemical Composition & Physical Properties</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Formula:</strong> $FeCr_2O_4$ ($FeO \\cdot Cr_2O_3$). Pure mineral theoretically contains <strong>68% chromic oxide ($Cr_2O_3$)</strong> and 32% iron oxide ($FeO$).</li>\n                <li><strong>Properties:</strong> Iron-black to brownish-black color, metallic to sub-metallic luster, high specific gravity (4.5 to 4.8), and extreme resistance to chemical corrosion, oxidation, and heat (melting point ~2,180°C).</li>\n                <li><strong>Geological Genesis:</strong> Magmatic cumulate mineral formed by fractional crystallization in ultramafic igneous rocks (dunites, peridotites, pyroxenites, and serpentinites).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Primary Industrial Applications</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Metallurgical Industry (Over 80% of Consumption):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Manufacturing <strong>Stainless Steel</strong>: Commercial stainless steel contains a <strong>minimum of 10.5% to 18% chromium</strong>. Chromium reacts spontaneously with atmospheric oxygen to form an invisible, self-healing, impermeable layer of <strong>Chromium Oxide ($Cr_2O_3$)</strong> on the metal surface, completely blocking rusting and acid corrosion.</li>\n                        <li>Manufacturing <strong>Ferro-Chrome (FeCr)</strong> and Charge Chrome in submerged electric arc furnaces.</li>\n                        <li>Nichrome ($Ni-Cr$) resistance heating wires, specialized armor plating, and gas turbine superalloys.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>2. Refractory Industry (10% to 15% of Consumption):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Chromite has an ultra-high melting point and thermal stability. Used to make chrome-magnesite and magnesite-chrome refractory bricks lining open-hearth steel furnaces, copper converters, and cement rotary kilns.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>3. Chemical Industry:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Production of sodium dichromate, chromic acid, and chromium sulfate used in <strong>leather tanning</strong>, electroplating, pigments (chrome yellow, chrome green), and wood preservatives.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
