            "title": "Refractory & Alloying Metals: Tungsten, Molybdenum & Vanadium in India",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Refractory & Alloying Metals: Tungsten, Molybdenum & Vanadium</h3>\n            <p><strong>Refractory metals</strong> are characterized by extraordinarily high melting points, superior hardness, and structural resilience at extreme temperatures. They are indispensable for advanced national defense, aerospace rocketry, and high-speed precision machining.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Tungsten ($W$ / Wolfram): The Heat Champion</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Properties:</strong> Possesses the <strong>highest melting point of all metallic elements (3,422°C)</strong>, highest tensile strength at high temperatures, and lowest coefficient of thermal expansion.</li>\n                <li><strong>Primary Ores:</strong> <strong>Wolframite</strong> ($(Fe,Mn)WO_4$) and <strong>Scheelite</strong> ($CaWO_4$).</li>\n                <li><strong>Strategic Applications:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Tungsten Carbide ($WC$):</strong> Ultra-hard material used in drilling bits, mining picks, metal-cutting machine tools, and dies.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Defense:</strong> Armor-piercing kinetic energy penetrators, artillery shell cores, and missile rocket nozzles.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Indian Deposits:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Degana (Nagaur district, Rajasthan):</strong> India’s most famous historic tungsten deposit, hosted in quartz veins cutting the Sewariya granite. (Mining is currently inactive; India imports ~100% of tungsten concentrates).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Balda (Sirohi district, Rajasthan)</strong> and <strong>Kuhi-Khobna (Nagpur, Maharashtra)</strong>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Molybdenum ($Mo$): Superalloy Toughness</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Enhances the strength, weldability, and corrosion resistance of structural steels at elevated temperatures. Used in aircraft engines, nuclear reactors, and petroleum cracking catalysts.</li>\n                <li><strong>Indian Occurrences:</strong> Associated with copper sulfide ores in <strong>Malanjkhand (Balaghat, MP)</strong> and shear zones in <strong>Harur-Uttangarai (Dharmapuri, Tamil Nadu)</strong> and <strong>Madurai/Dindigul</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Vanadium ($V$): High-Strength Low-Alloy (HSLA) Steels</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Small additions of vanadium (0.05% to 0.15%) double the yield strength of carbon steel, creating <strong>HSLA steels</strong> used in high-pressure oil pipelines, suspension bridges, and railway axles.</li>\n                <li><strong>Vanadium Redox Flow Batteries (VRFB):</strong> Emerging utility-scale energy storage technology for renewable solar and wind grids.</li>\n                <li><strong>Indian Deposits:</strong> Hosted in <strong>Titaniferous Vanadiferous Magnetite (TVM)</strong> ores in <strong>Mayurbhanj and Keonjhar (Odisha)</strong>, <strong>Shimoga and Hassan (Karnataka)</strong>, and newly discovered occurrences in the <strong>Palaeozoic carbonaceous phyllites of Arunachal Pradesh (Depo and Tamen areas)</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Tungsten has the highest melting point of all metals (3,422°C) and is used in cemented carbide cutting tools and armor penetrators.",
                "Degana (Nagaur district, Rajasthan) is India's most famous historic wolframite deposit.",
                "Molybdenum strengthens structural steels and occurs in Malanjkhand (MP) and Dharmapuri (Tamil Nadu).",
                "Vanadium doubles steel yield strength in HSLA steels and is vital for Vanadium Redox Flow Batteries.",
                "Vanadium occurs in Titaniferous Vanadiferous Magnetite (TVM) ores in Odisha (Mayurbhanj) and Arunachal Pradesh."
            ],
