            "id": "m17",
            "title": "Titanium Minerals: Ilmenite, Rutile & Beach Sand Heavy Minerals of India",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Titanium Minerals: Ilmenite, Rutile & The Placer Beach Sands</h3>\n            <p><strong>Titanium ($Ti$)</strong> is a lustrous transition metal celebrated for having the highest strength-to-weight ratio of any metal, complete resistance to seawater and chlorine corrosion, and biocompatibility. India possesses some of the world's largest and richest deposits of titanium minerals, concentrated along its vast tropical coastlines as heavy mineral placer beach sands.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Primary Titanium Minerals</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Ilmenite ($FeTiO_3$):</strong> Iron titanium oxide. Black to brownish-black; the principal commercial ore of titanium, containing <strong>50% to 60% titanium dioxide ($TiO_2$)</strong>. Accounts for over 90% of global titanium mineral supply.</li>\n                <li><strong>Rutile ($TiO_2$):</strong> Natural titanium dioxide. Reddish-brown to black; purer ore containing <strong>90% to 95% $TiO_2$</strong>. Commands higher market prices and is used directly in welding electrode flux and titanium sponge production.</li>\n                <li><strong>Leucoxene:</strong> Weathered, high-$TiO_2$ alteration product of ilmenite.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Primary Industrial Applications</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. White Pigment Manufacturing (>90% of Consumption):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Over 90% of global titanium minerals are converted into pure synthetic <strong>Titanium Dioxide ($TiO_2$) pigment</strong> via the sulfate or chloride process.</li>\n                        <li>Possesses the highest refractive index of any white material (higher than diamond!), imparting opacity, whiteness, and UV protection to <strong>paints, architectural coatings, plastics, paper, and sunscreens</strong>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>2. Aerospace, Defense & Marine Engineering:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Titanium Sponge & Alloys:</strong> Used for jet engine compressor blades, airframe structural beams, spacecraft casings, submarine hulls, and desalination plant heat exchangers.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Biomedical:</strong> Surgical bone pins, artificial joint replacements, and dental implants (non-toxic and integrates directly with bone).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. India’s Placer Beach Sand Deposits & Coastal Belts</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>India accounts for approximately <strong>15% to 20% of the world's total ilmenite resources</strong>. Coastal sands are naturally sorted by marine wave action and longshore drift, creating heavy mineral concentrations:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Kerala Coast:</strong> Chavara deposit in Kollam district; one of the richest ilmenite beach placers in the world, operated by IREL and Kerala Minerals and Metals Limited (KMML).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Tamil Nadu Coast:</strong> Manavalakurichi (Kanyakumari district) and coastal Tirunelveli/Tuticorin.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Odisha Coast:</strong> Chhatrapur deposit in Ganjam district (Odisha Sands Complex - OSCOM), operated by IREL.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Andhra Pradesh Coast:</strong> Extensive sand dunes along Srikakulam (Bhavanapadu) and Visakhapatnam.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Ilmenite ($FeTiO_3$) and Rutile ($TiO_2$) are the primary commercial ores of titanium.",
                "India holds 15%–20% of global ilmenite resources in coastal heavy mineral beach sands.",
                "Over 90% of titanium is consumed as white $TiO_2$ pigment for paints, plastics, and paper.",
                "Titanium metal has the highest strength-to-weight ratio and supreme corrosion resistance for aerospace and marine hulls.",
                "Key beach sand deposits: Chavara (Kollam, Kerala), Manavalakurichi (Tamil Nadu), and Chhatrapur / OSCOM (Odisha).",
                "Operated by public enterprises: IREL (under DAE) and KMML (Kerala)."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "India’s coastal beach sand placers (Chavara, Manavalakurichi, OSCOM) host up to 20% of world ilmenite, supplying raw material for $TiO_2$ pigments and strategic aerospace titanium sponge."
            ],
