            "title": "Comprehensive Review & Comparative Data: Ferrous Metallic Minerals Matrix",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Ferrous Metallic Minerals: Comprehensive Synthesis & Exam Matrix</h3>\n            <p>Ferrous metallic minerals constitute the industrial foundation of India's heavy manufacturing economy. The spatial distribution, state reserve and production rankings, and global positions of Iron Ore, Manganese, Chromite, and Nickel form a vital core of civil services economic geography.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Comprehensive Examination Matrix</h4>\n            <table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 10px; width: 100%;\">\n                <tr style=\"background-color: #f0f0f0;\">\n                    <th>Mineral</th>\n                    <th>Chief Indian Reserves Hierarchy</th>\n                    <th>Chief Indian Production Hierarchy</th>\n                    <th>Global Rankings & Standing</th>\n                    <th>Prime Mines / Regions</th>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>Iron Ore</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Karnataka &gt; Odisha &gt; Jharkhand &gt; Chhattisgarh</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Odisha &gt; Chhattisgarh &gt; Karnataka &gt; Jharkhand</strong></td>\n                    <td>\n                        • <strong>Reserves:</strong> Australia &gt; Brazil &gt; Russia &gt; China &gt; India (5th)<br>\n                        • <strong>Production:</strong> Australia &gt; Brazil &gt; India &gt; China (<strong>India 3rd globally!</strong>)\n                    </td>\n                    <td>\n                        • <strong>Odisha:</strong> Keonjhar (Joda, Barbil, Kiriburu), Mayurbhanj (Badampahar, Gorumahisani), Sundargarh (Bonai)<br>\n                        • <strong>Jharkhand:</strong> West Singhbhum (Noamundi, Badaburu, Pansiraburu, Chiria)<br>\n                        • <strong>Chhattisgarh:</strong> Dantewada (Bailadila), Durg (Dalli-Rajhara), Bilaspur<br>\n                        • <strong>Karnataka:</strong> Chikmagalur (Bababudan, Kudremukh), Bellary (Sandur, Donimalai)<br>\n                        • <strong>Goa:</strong> Adulmale, Kudnam, Bicholim<br>\n                        • <strong>Maharashtra:</strong> Chandrapur, Surjagarh, Nagpur, Bhandara, Ratnagiri<br>\n                        • <strong>MP:</strong> Balaghat, Chhindwara, Jabalpur, Jhabua<br>\n                        • <strong>AP:</strong> Ongole, Guntur\n                    </td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>Manganese</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Odisha (~44%) &gt; Karnataka (~22%) &gt; MP (~12%) &gt; Maharashtra (~7%)</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Madhya Pradesh (~33%) &gt; Maharashtra (~26%) &gt; Odisha (~20%)</strong></td>\n                    <td>Consistently among the top global producers alongside South Africa, Australia, and Gabon.</td>\n                    <td>\n                        • <strong>MP:</strong> Balaghat (Bharveli - Asia's largest underground manganese mine), Chhindwara<br>\n                        • <strong>Maharashtra:</strong> Nagpur (Mansar, Kandri), Bhandara (Dongri Buzurg - battery peroxide ore)<br>\n                        • <strong>Odisha:</strong> Keonjhar (Jamda-Koira), Sundargarh<br>\n                        • <strong>Operator:</strong> MOIL Limited (>50% of output)\n                    </td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>Chromite</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Odisha (&gt;95% of total reserves)</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Odisha (~99% of total production)</strong></td>\n                    <td>Among top 4 global producers alongside South Africa, Kazakhstan, and Turkey.</td>\n                    <td>\n                        • <strong>Odisha:</strong> Sukinda Valley (Jajpur district - absolute national monopoly); Boula-Nuasahi (Keonjhar - PGE-associated chromite)<br>\n                        • <strong>Others:</strong> Hassan (Karnataka), Singhbhum (Jharkhand)\n                    </td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>Nickel & Cobalt</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Odisha (&gt;92% of conditional nickel resources)</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Zero commercial primary metal extraction</strong> (100% refined import dependent).</td>\n                    <td>DRC produces >70% of global cobalt; Indonesia and Philippines dominate nickel.</td>\n                    <td>\n                        • <strong>Odisha:</strong> Sukinda Valley (nickeliferous limonite overburden, 0.4%–0.9% Ni)<br>\n                        • Associated traces in Malanjkhand copper (MP) and Singhbhum (Jharkhand)\n                    </td>\n                </tr>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4>2. Key Macroeconomic Themes</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>National Steel Policy 2017:</strong> 300 MT crude steel capacity target by 2030–31 requiring ~480 MT iron ore annually.</li>\n                <li><strong>India’s Global Position:</strong> India is the <strong>3rd largest producer of Iron Ore</strong>, <strong>2nd largest producer of Crude Steel</strong>, and the <strong>#1 producer of Direct Reduced Iron (Sponge Iron)</strong> in the world!</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
